%N A398323 Number of self-avoiding walks between two antipodal vertices on a hexagon of size n in the triangular grid.
%C A398323 On the Cartesian grid this can be defined as the lattice T = {(x,y): 0 <= x,y <= 2*n and |x - y| <= n}.  We consider the number of self avoiding walks between opposite corners.
%C A398323 The number of vertices and edges in the graph are A003215(n) and A045945(n) respectively.
%e A398323 The graphs for n = 1 and 2 are illustrated below:
%e A398323                      o---o---o
%e A398323                     / \ / \ / \
%e A398323       o---o        o---o---o---o
%e A398323      / \ / \      / \ / \ / \ / \
%e A398323     o---o---o    o---o---o---o---o
%e A398323      \ / \ /      \ / \ / \ / \ /
%e A398323       o---o        o---o---o---o
%e A398323                     \ / \ / \ /
%e A398323                      o---o---o
%e A398323 For n = 1, the graph is the wheel graph W_7, whose vertices may be considered as a hexagon and an internal point. The number of self-avoiding walks between two opposite vertices of the hexagon is 21. If the outer vertices are labeled 1,2,3,4,5,6 and the internal vertex 7, then walks between say 1 and 4 are: 174, 1734, 1754, 17234, 17654, 1274, 12754, 127654, 12734, 1234, 12374, 1237654, 123754, 1674, 16734, 167234, 16754, 1654, 16574, 1657234, 165734.
%o A398323 (Python)
%o A398323 from graphillion import GraphSet
%o A398323 import networkx as nx
%o A398323 verts = {(x,y) for x in range(2*N+1) for y in range(2*N+1) if abs(x-y)<=N}
%o A398323 G = nx.Graph()
%o A398323 G.add_nodes_from(verts)
%o A398323 for (x,y) in verts:
%o A398323     adj = {(x+1,y),(x-1,y),(x,y+1),(x,y-1),(x+1,y+1),(x-1,y-1)}.intersection(verts)
%o A398323     G.add_edges_from([((x,y),a) for a in adj])
%o A398323 GraphSet.set_universe(G.edges)
%o A398323 paths = GraphSet.paths((0,0),(2*N,2*N))
%o A398323 len(paths)
%Y A398323 Cf. A003215, A007764, A045945, A271741, A343307, A351994, A356610.
%K A398323 nonn,more,new
